        <section id="how-does-working-with-others-within-an-organisation-support-cyber-security?"  class="t-bg-primary">
          
            <h2>How does working with others within an organisation support cyber security?</h2>
              <p>When it comes to cyber security it is important that all employees are aware of the risks and are onboard with any strategies in place. It is therefore imperative that cyber security teams work with others in organisations to make this happen.</p>
<p>Scroll through the slides below to see why these skills are important.</p>

              
              
<div class="gallery-container">
  <div role="group" class="gallery no-js">
    <ul>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Finance
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>The finance team are often one of the most important departments to support cyber security within a business as they are the ones that have access to the money. Senior management may make the decisions but organising the finances involves the finance team. They also need to work with the cyber security department to make sure that all security protocols are in place within their own department understanding the financial consequences if not.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/cs/cs_u6_s2_p1_slide1.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Operations
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>Security is about operational processes as well as people and technology. Cyber security professionals need to work with the operations team to understanding how the business operates and identify any in security in processes both physical and technological.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/cs/cs_u6_s2_p1_slide2.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Human Resources
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>The Human Resources team (HR) are a key ally to the cyber security team.  HR will help ensure that the rest of the business know and understand their responsibilities to cyber security as well as to help roll out training and awareness events.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/cs/cs_u6_s2_p1_slide3.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Legal
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>The legal team are important in helping inform what legal responsibilities to data use, security and protection are. Regulations such as GDPR for example should be built into the policies and training for all departments.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/cs/cs_u6_s2_p1_slide4.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Sales and Marketing
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>Good security practice is an excellent selling point in today’s world whatever the industry. The cyber security team should work with the sales and marketing team to make sure that the benefits of this are fully communicated to all customers and linked businesses.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/cs/cs_u6_s2_p1_slide5.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</div>

            
          
